[?2004hroot@ff6c5ad93275:/app# cat >> README.md <<'MD'
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Resume and durability
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Each checkpoint records the last committed document ID, the committed row count,
[?2004l[?2004h> and the last shard's byte offset and row count. The worker flushes and fsyncs all
[?2004l[?2004h> rows (including shards closed during rotation), syncs the index directory, then
[?2004l[?2004h> writes/fsyncs a temporary checkpoint and atomically replaces the checkpoint file.
[?2004l[?2004h> The checkpoint directory is synced after replacement. Source iteration resumes
[?2004l[?2004h> strictly **after** the committed ID; IDs need not be contiguous.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> At startup, the worker truncates the last shard to the committed offset and
[?2004l[?2004h> removes later shards. This discards any uncommitted suffix, including a torn JSON
[?2004l[?2004h> line left by SIGKILL. Recovery is repeatable if interrupted. SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP
[?2004l[?2004h> request a cooperative stop: finish the current document and commit the partial
[?2004l[?2004h> batch. A forced kill may replay uncommitted work, but never skips committed work.
[?2004l[?2004h> `./jobs status` counts only committed rows, not the speculative suffix.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Checkpoints from the old, unsafe protocol have no durable boundary and trigger a
[?2004l[?2004h> one-time rebuild. Malformed/unsupported new checkpoints or a truncated committed
[?2004l[?2004h> shard fail rather than silently discarding acknowledged progress. Do not edit a
[?2004l[?2004h> checkpoint to advance it manually. The corpus must remain stable during a build;
[?2004l[?2004h> use `./jobs clean` to start a new build after corpus changes. Normal supervised
[?2004l[?2004h> runs are serialized by the supervisor lock.
[?2004l[?2004h> MD
